+"URL": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6483198", "abstract": "<jats:p>&lt;div&gt;\n &lt;span&gt;Institutional 
+tokenization of US securities is proceeding through three distinct strategies, each synthesizing 
+infrastructure from different combinations of regulated entities. Strategy 1 (the Regulated Collateral 
+Circuit) constructs a closed loop where DTC-tokenized Treasuries serve as 24/7 margin collateral, settled in 
+bank-issued deposit tokens, with clearing through Ubyx. Strategy 2 (the Multi-Issuer Yield Stack) composes a 
+product holding tokenized Treasuries from five issuers with a smart redemption router that eliminates the 
+single-pool dependency identified in the Minimum Viable Equivalence Pack (MVEP) framework. Strategy 3 (the 
+Tokenized Equity Rails) builds the complete issuance-to-trading pipeline for tokenized US equities using 
+SEC-approved exchange infrastructure from Nasdaq (approved March 18, 2026) and NYSE (Securitize MoU, March 24, 
+2026).&lt;/span&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n &lt;br&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n This paper maps each 
+strategy against the MVEP’s ten categories, develops nine strategy-specific stress scenarios traced through 
+MVEP category failure chains, analyzes the cross-strategy concentration of Securitize as the system-of-record 
+operator across all three strategies, identifies the corporate actions reconciliation gap as Strategy 3’s most 
+novel failure mode, develops an infrastructure competition framework for the emerging deposit-token market 
+(JPMD, BNY deposits, Citi deposits, with Ubyx clearing), and specifies observable phase gates with 
+quantitative thresholds gating each strategy transition. The analysis draws on the Control Layer Intensity 
+Index (CLII) and MVEP frameworks developed in companion papers (Zukowski, 2026a; Zukowski, 2026b; Zukowski, 
+2026c).\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n &lt;br&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n Four findings emerge. First, each 
+strategy satisfies different MVEP categories with different strength: Strategy 1 is strongest on custody and 
+settlement finality (DTC + Canton atomic settlement), Strategy 2 is strongest on collateral operability 
+(redundant multi-pool redemption), and Strategy 3 is strongest on rights parity (SEC-mandated identical 
+rights) but weakest on reconciliation (the corporate actions gap). Second, Securitize’s presence as transfer 
+agent across all three strategies creates a system-level concentration risk that entity-level CLII scores do 
+not capture: a 72-hour Securitize operational failure would degrade all three strategies simultaneously. 
+Third, the corporate actions reconciliation gap (record date, ex-date, and stock split problems in 24/7 
+tokenized equity trading) requires one of three architectures (trading halt, blockchain-as-authoritative, or 
+dual-record synchronization), each with distinct MVEP implications. Fourth, the deposit-token market (JPMD, 
+BNY, Citi) is the control-layer competition applied to institutional settlement: the entity that issues the 
+dominant deposit token captures the settlement-layer rent, and Ubyx’s clearing function determines whether 
+this becomes a monopoly or a competitive market.\n&lt;/div&gt;</jats:p>", "author": [ { "ORCID": 
