+"URL": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6483118", "abstract": "<jats:p>&lt;div&gt;\n Five dollar-denominated 
+digital products share a denomination and a target price. They share little else of relevance to institutional 
+diligence. USDC, USDT, and USDG are payment stablecoins issued under three different regulatory regimes (US 
+state money transmitter licenses, British Virgin Islands law, and the Monetary Authority of Singapore 
+framework). DAI is an overcollateralized stablecoin with no issuer, maintained by smart contract governance. 
+OUSG is a tokenized Treasury wrapper structured as a Delaware limited partnership. Each product presents as a 
+“dollar,” yet each requires a structurally different diligence package for institutional 
+adoption.\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n &lt;br&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n This paper develops a 
+four-dimension taxonomy (legal claim type, system of record architecture, insolvency regime, and collateral 
+operability profile) that classifies these products and predicts which categories of the Minimum Viable 
+Equivalence Pack (MVEP) diligence framework are binding for each type. The paper extends the Control Layer 
+Intensity Index (CLII) to all five operators, maps the contagion dependencies across the product set, 
+stress-tests each product against the Silicon Valley Bank (SVB) scenario applied to current structures, and 
+constructs an eight-metric collateral eligibility framework.\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n 
+&lt;br&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n Six findings emerge. First, binding MVEP categories are predictable 
+from the four-dimension classification. Second, operator regulation intensity and product equivalence are 
+separate dimensions that require separate measurement: Circle scored highest on the CLII (0.92) and 
+experienced a 13% depeg during SVB; Tether scored lower (0.52) and maintained its peg. Third, CLII score and 
+collateral eligibility can move in opposite directions, with USDT scoring higher than USDC on eligibility 
+despite lower regulatory intensity, because USDT has no documented stress depeg. Fourth, contagion flows from 
+simple, liquid payment stablecoins to complex products that hold them, the opposite of the direction commonly 
+assumed in traditional finance, with evidence from two production events (SVB March 2023, Circle pool incident 
+March 2025). Fifth, each product has traded its SVB-era concentration risk for a different one: the 
+vulnerability migrated but did not disappear. Sixth, the product with the most liquidation paths (OUSG, three) 
+has the worst collateral eligibility; path count is misleading, and path quality determines 
+eligibility.\n&lt;/div&gt;</jats:p>", "author": [ { "ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-3642-2450", 
