-"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6412731", "abstract": "<jats:p>Dollar stablecoins extend the dollar’s exchange, 
-store-of-value, and funding functions into blockchain markets, largely outside the Federal Reserve’s standard 
-supervisory and reporting perimeter, though not necessarily outside all federal or state oversight. We argue 
-the policy-relevant unit of analysis is not the token but the gateway (the issuer, exchange, or protocol that 
-routes stablecoin value) because the same dollar token inherits different compliance, monetary, and safe-haven 
-properties depending on which gateway processes it.Using daily Ethereum gateway transfer volumes (19 entities, 
-51 addresses, 27.7 percent of USDC/USDT value), monthly counterparty networks (15 gateways, 35 months, 3,804 
-unique dyads), and stress-episode windows around the Silicon Valley Bank (SVB) crisis and the Binance USD 
-(BUSD) regulatory action, we report three descriptive findings.First, stablecoin supply is cointegrated with 
-Fed balance-sheet variables in a regime-dependent way; a Treasury bill minus on-chain lending-rate spread 
-provides mechanism-consistent opportunity-cost evidence. The relationship is dollar-specific, absent for 
-Bitcoin or Ethereum. Cointegration is strongest during quantitative tightening (trace = 48.18) and weakens 
-during easing (trace = 17.71) even as level correlations remain strong (r = -0.98).Second, that co-movement 
-distributes unevenly across the gateway layer: institutional gateways (both regulated and offshore) share the 
-aggregate inverse relationship with Fed assets while permissionless protocols trend in the opposite direction, 
-and the routing network is concentrating; volume doubled over three years while unique counterparties declined 
-25 percent and cross-gateway counterparties fell from 5.8 to 2.6 percent.Third, within the dollar-pegged 
-settlement instruments we observe, stress behavior is gateway-contingent: capital routes across gateways 
-rather than out of dollar-pegged stablecoins, with tier share recovery coinciding with the SVB intervention 
-sequence and the restoration of USDC convertibility, though causal attribution across intervention tools is 
-not identified. Fragmentation manifests primarily as competition and concentration within the dollar’s own 
-gateway infrastructure, rather than as substitution into non-dollar-pegged instruments. For monitoring 
-purposes, gateway-level flow data are more informative than token-level aggregates for assessing regulatory 
-exposure, concentration risk, and stress routing.</jats:p>", "author": [ { "affiliation": [], "family": 
