-"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6352118", "abstract": "<jats:p>When a token network's adaptive reward controller 
-mistakes a temporary infrastructure outage for a permanent loss of operators, it over-compensates with higher 
-rewards. This over-reaction accidentally builds a financial buffer that protects the network when a real 
-economic shock arrives. This counterintuitive finding emerges from a 624-run agent-based simulation of a DePIN 
-token economy extended with an operational risk layer (infrastructure outages, key losses, correlated software 
-failures, and regulatory disruptions) calibrated to empirical failure rates across Ethereum, Helium, Geodnet, 
-and Filecoin. Under competitor shock, the adaptive controller reduces worst-case outcome variance by 6.89×, up 
-from 6.25× without operational risk. The analytical prediction was that infrastructure failures would make the 
-controller less effective at stabilizing the network; instead, they make it more effective. Fixedrate reward 
-schedules, which cannot adjust to any source of disruption, perform even worse (outcome variability increases 
-from 0.544 to 0.569), widening the adaptive controller's advantage. A second finding reveals a design 
-vulnerability not apparent from economic-only simulation. Reputation systems that gate recovery on a 
-performance threshold break when routine failures make that threshold unachievable. At 0.5% daily downtime, an 
-operator has only a 16% chance of clearing the recovery threshold in any given quarter. The result: average 
-reputation decays to 0.18 on a 0-5 scale, effectively disabling reputationweighted governance. The collapse is 
-structural; no adjustment to the decay rate fixes it, but lowering the recovery threshold to 0.95 restores 
-stability. The operational risk taxonomy draws on two emerging standards: the Validator Operation Standard 
-(ValOS), with 74 active risk vectors and 64 testable controls, and the Node Operator Risk Standard (NORS), 
-providing institutional certification across 31 control objectives. Lido V3 stVaults, which set each 
-operator's collateral requirements based on their risk score, demonstrate that ValOS-aligned risk assessment 
-already governs economic outcomes on Ethereum mainnet. The simulation methodology provides a template for 
-extending other agent-based token simulations with operational risk.</jats:p>", "author": [ { "affiliation": 
