-"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6267698", "abstract": "<jats:p>&lt;p&gt;Dollar stablecoins extend the dollar’s 
-exchange, store-of-value, and funding functions into blockchain markets, largely outside the Federal Reserve’s 
-standard supervisory and reporting perimeter. We argue the policy-relevant unit of analysis is the 
-&lt;i&gt;gateway&lt;/i&gt;&amp;nbsp;(the issuer, exchange, or protocol that routes stablecoin value) because 
-the same dollar token inherits different compliance, monetary, and safe-haven properties depending on which 
-gateway processes it.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Three findings:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;First, 
-stablecoin supply is cointegrated with Fed balance-sheet variables in a regime-dependent way; a Treasury bill 
-minus on-chain lending-rate spread provides mechanism-consistent opportunity-cost evidence. The relationship 
-is dollar-specific, absent for Bitcoin or Ethereum. Cointegration is strongest during quantitative tightening 
-and weakens during easing even as level correlations remain high.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Second, the long-run 
-co-movement distributes unevenly across the gateway layer.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Third, within the dollar-pegged 
-settlement instruments we observe, stress behavior is gateway-contingent.&lt;/p&gt;</jats:p>", "author": [ { 
